    infoPageBlock1Html:
      "<p class=\"infoPage__lead\"><strong class=\"infoPage__exhibitTitle\">&ldquo;Doing Well, Don&rsquo;t Worry&rdquo;: Short Tales of Women&rsquo;s Work and Mobility</strong> was originally <a href=\"https://wmf.org.eg/en/events/women-and-memory-forums-exhibition-doing-well-dont-worry-short-tales-of-womens-work-and-mobility/\" class=\"infoPage__link\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">exhibited</a> at the American University in Cairo in May 2017. It was powered by international collaboration with the Women and Memory Forum (EG), Women&rsquo;s Museum in Denmark (DK), Danish-Egyptian Dialogue Institute (EG/DK), Anthropology Unit and the Cynthia Nelson Institute from the American University in Cairo (EG), the Tiraz Centre (JO) and the Knowledge Workshop (LE).</p>",
    infoPageBlock2Html:
      "<p class=\"infoPage__body\">In March 2026, the Women and Memory Forum hosted students part of Georgetown University&rsquo;s (USA) Centennial Lab, ARST 4478, <em>Heritage and Development in the Arab World</em>. Students learned about feminist heritage in Egypt, the WMF&rsquo;s challenges, their community efforts, and their archival practices. Engaging Georgetown&rsquo;s Jesuit values of &ldquo;people for others&rdquo; and &ldquo;community in diversity,&rdquo; they wished to take their learning outside of the classroom. Out of this sentiment, this digital archive was born.</p>",
    infoPageCarouselCaption: "Photos from the May 2017 exhibition at AUC.",
    bibliographyTitle: "Site Bibliography",
    infoPageCarouselAria: "Photographs from the May 2017 exhibition at the American University in Cairo",
    storymapWelcomeBody:
      "Feminism is made through relationships. “Doing Well, Don’t Worry” is a testament to these connections: letters exchanged across generations, a teacher’s documents. As you enter the archive, we invite you to recognize the dense web — the letters, the lessons, the names passed quietly from woman to woman — from which feminist practice grows. What women have built, they have built together.",
    storymapWelcomeCta: "enter the archive",
    homeLetterDateStamp: "Egypt · 1978",
    homeLetterLede: "A letter to her daughter Reem Saadi — written from prison on toilet paper.",
    homeLetterBodyHtml:
      "<p>My dear daughter Reem,</p>" +
      "<p>I send you on your birthday my love and wishes for happy days to come, more joyful and bright. How I wish I could have been beside you in this special day when you are becoming sixteen and making your way forward, very competently as a colleague whom we all feel proud to have. I promise your next birthday that I would either be with you or you would be here with me. In order for you to not to worry, I live in a very cute room which you would like. It is 2.5 meters x 1.5 meters with very high ceiling (almost 7 meters) with a window (75cmx50cm) with iron bars and wooden shutters behind it. The room&rsquo;s door is an iron one with a little opening, with iron bars, which I block at night with newspaper papers. I have a single (camping) bed, like the one you wanted, the rent of this bed with a mattress is 15pt per day.</p>" +
      "<p>As for the prison, it is free, thanks be to them. The floor has a plastic mat with a foam mattress which I use as a sitting room. The bathroom is one step away from the bed. It has a bucket for urine, a water bucket and a plastic container.</p>" +
      "<p>I also have newspapers, some books and a radio which I keep very close as it reminds me of all of you and gives the news of the world. I especially listen to &lsquo;The Voice of Arab Egypt&rsquo; which broadcasted the news of my arrest. What made me very anxious, but also happy was the voice of dear Safy (Safinza Kazem, Egyptian journalist and writer) singing with Sheikh Imam &ldquo;Rise Egypt and be strong.&rdquo; I felt as if she was talking to me alone, and I wept, being both pained and happy: pained because she is far away, and happy to hear her tender, wonderful voice.</p>" +
      "<p>After all these tempting things, you would probably want come and stay with me, especially you can save your effort as you always say you want. The question is what would you do with all that saved effort?</p>" +
      "<p>It&rsquo;s a wondrous journey indeed, but I feel this letter is getting too long and still I didn&rsquo;t write all that I wanted to write about, given that I am running huge risks to obtain pen and paper. I had wished for this letter to reach you on your birthday, however, this delay was out of my hands.</p>" +
      "<p>Regards and kisses for your mother, father and Sohit. Kisses to all loved ones without mentioning names. Also regards and kisses to your grandmother, your two aunts and your cousins, and wish to meet you soon. Aunty Hanem, or Hanem only since we have become colleagues</p>" +
      "<p>December 1978.</p>" +
      "<p>Doing well, don&rsquo;t worry.</p>",
